          "worked_solution": "The *truth set* of a predicate $P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})$ is the set of $n$-tuples in its universal set $U={U}_{1}\\times {U}_{2}\\times ⋯\\times {U}_{n}$ such that substituting these tuples for the variables makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\in U\\,|\\,P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U={R}^{2}.$\n\nWe need to find $(x,y)\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-36>0$\n\nLet's first rearrange our inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-36 & >0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & >36 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{36} & >1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that this represents an ellipse centered at $O$ with semi-axes $3$ and $2.$\n\nFinally, let's sketch the truth set $T$ of $P(x,y):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14129781/assets/9658f04618246f6c35ab0d22c7998accb5708791cdfc756cd276654b7e8fb18a.png)",
